    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n21515" root="شأو" body-key="$">
    
      3 
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَآءَاهُ</span></b> : see 1. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يُشَاوِى</span> occurs in a verse of
Milhah El-Jarmee, meaning <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يُسَابِقُ</span>, from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَأْوٌ</span>
meaning <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">طَلَقٌ</span>: one says <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَأَاهُ</span>, aor. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَشْأَاهُ</span>, meaning
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">سَبَقَهُ</span>: but the verb of the measure regularly formed
from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الشَّأْوُ</span> is <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">شَآءى</span>; so that <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يشاوى</span> is formed by
transposition and by the change of the <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ء</span> into <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ى</span>.
(Ham p. 786.)

